1. Create a variable that stores the current active scene's name or ID. This will help you keep track of what is currently being displayed on screen.
2. Whenever you want to transition from one scene to another, update this variable with the new scene's identifier. You can use an enum for this purpose if your app has only a few fixed scenes.
3. Use conditional statements based on the value stored in the variable to determine which scene should be rendered by the `renderScene` method. For example:
```javascript
const renderScene = () => {
    switch(currentActiveScene) {
        case 'sceneOne': return <SceneOne />;
        break;
        case 'sceneTwo': return <SceneTwo />;
        break;
        default: return null; // Fallback option
}
};
return (
<View>
{renderScene()}
</View>
);
```
4. To trigger a scene change, simply set the `currentActiveScene` variable to the desired scene's identifier and call `forceUpdate()` to re-render the component tree.
5. If you need more complex logic when switching between scenes, consider using React Navigation or other routing libraries designed specifically for managing navigation within React Native apps. These libraries provide features like animated transitions, stack management, and route configuration out of the box.
